lesson: 143
title: Dividing by a one-digit number: how many can be bought
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: When you know the money and the price of one thing, divide to find how many things it buys: quantity = cost ÷ price.
explain: To divide a big number by a one-digit number, divide the hundreds, then the tens, then the ones: 576 ÷ 4: 5 hundreds ÷ 4 = 1 hundred, 1 hundred left; 17 tens ÷ 4 = 4 tens, 1 ten left; 16 ones ÷ 4 = 4. So 576 ÷ 4 = 144.
explain: Check by multiplying back: 144 × 4 = 576.
vars: a=name p=3..9 q=12..140
let: c = p * q
need: c >= 100 && c <= 999
answer: q
exercise: {a} has {c} dollars. One book costs {p} dollars. How many books can {a.he} buy with all the money?
exercise: A school spent {c} dollars on balls. Each ball cost {p} dollars. How many balls did the school buy?
exercise: {c} eggs are packed in boxes of {p}. How many boxes are filled?
exercise: A farm sold {c} kg of potatoes in bags of {p} kg each. How many bags did it sell?
exercise: {c} children are split into {p} equal teams. How many children are in each team?
exercise: A ribbon {c} cm long is cut into {p} equal pieces. How long is each piece?
test: The price of a ticket is {p} dollars, and {a} paid {c} dollars for tickets for the whole school. How many tickets did {a.he} buy?
test: How many vases are needed for {c} roses if each vase holds {p} roses?
test: A gardener has {c} litres of water and pours {p} litres on each tree. How many trees get water?
test: {p} buses took {c} children to the museum, the same number in each bus. How many children were in one bus?
exercise: How many books can {a} buy with {c} dollars, if one book costs {p} dollars?
exercise: {c} dollars, {p} dollars per book. How many books?
exercise: A school in Ohio spent {c} dollars on chairs at {p} dollars apiece. How many chairs did it get?
exercise: {a} has {c} cents and a sticker costs {p} cents. How many stickers can {a.he} buy with all of it?
exercise: {c} apples are packed into bags of {p} at a farm in Kent. How many bags are filled?
exercise: A teacher in Toronto splits {c} pencils equally among {p} classes. How many pencils does each class get?
exercise: A rope {c} cm long is cut into {p} pieces of equal length. How many centimetres is one piece?
exercise: {a} spends {c} dollars on games in Sydney, and every game costs {p} dollars. How many games does {a.he} buy?
test: Tickets for a fair in Iowa are {p} dollars each. How many tickets does {c} dollars pay for?
test: {p} classes in Wellington collected {c} cans, every class the same number. How many cans did one class collect?
test: How many rows of {p} seats can be made from {c} seats?
test: {c} litres of milk are poured into {p} equal tanks on a farm in Wales. How many litres go into each tank?
test: With {c} dollars, {a} buys as many {p}-dollar kites as {a.he} can and spends it all. How many kites does {a.he} buy?
test: A baker in Cork packs {c} muffins, {p} to a box. Find the number of boxes.
exercise: {a} got {c} dollars, book's {p} each, how many can {a.he} buy
exercise: school spent {c} dollars on balls, {p+1} — sorry, {p} dollars each. how many balls
exercise: my brother says {c} eggs in boxes of {p} is {q+2} boxes. nah. how many boxes really
exercise: {c} eggs, boxes of {p}, the hens are like 2 years old. how many boxes fill up
test: tickets {p} dollars each, i got {c} dollars. how many tickets
test: {c} dollars, {p} per toy, how many toys we can get
test: {a} reckons {c} bucks at {p} each buys {c-p}. thats wrong innit? how many really
exam: It is given that {a} holds {c} dollars and that a single book is priced at {p} dollars. Determine the number of books purchasable upon expenditure of the entire sum.
exam: Whereas a school expended {c} dollars upon balls, each ball being priced at {p} dollars, ascertain the number of balls so acquired.
exam: A quantity of {c} eggs is packed into boxes of {p.words} eggs each. State the number of boxes so filled.
exam: Compute the quotient of {c} divided by {p}.
exercise: Grandpa passed away and left the school {c} dollars for books. One book costs {p} dollars. How many books can the school buy with all the money?
exercise: Money's a bit tight at {a}'s house, so the family has exactly {c} dollars for food this month. A bag of groceries costs {p} dollars. How many bags can they buy?
test: {a} reckons {a.he}'s "between jobs" — ha, sacked more like. got {c} dollars left, chips are {p} dollars a bag, how many bags can the lazy git buy
